  "query_preview": "..Today...Clouds will be on the increase through the \nday as moisture begins to stream into the region from a weakening \nlow over southern California. A bit cooler temperatures and \nbreezier conditions are expected as a result this afternoon. Dry \nconditions should prevail for much of the day, however a few \nisolated showers and possibly a thunderstorm will be possible as \nthe day progresses. \n\nTonight into Saturday...A secondary through sags southward through \nthe Great Basin into Arizona. As the assocaited upper-level jet \nmoves overhead, the potential for for showers and thunderstorms \nbegins to increase. The greatest confidence in precipitation looks \nto mainly be along and north of the I-40 corridor as the better \nforcing looks to be further north. Coverage looks to peak overnight \ninto Saturday morning, before diminishing from west to east through \nthe afternoon hours.\n\nQPF looks to be on the lighter side given a lack of any robust \nforcing. Though a brief period of steadier precipitation looks \npossible Saturday morning with a transient band of frontogenetic \nforcing. All and all, amounts look to generally be around 0.25-0.50 \ninches across the higher terrain, with some locally higher amounts \nin the more favored upslope areas. Across the lower elevations, a \nfew hundredths to a tenth a best seem likely.\n\nSnowfall amounts look to generally be confined to areas above 7500-\n8000 feet, as 700 mb temperatures remain around 0C to start before \nfalling to -2C Saturday morning. Snow accumulations look to be \naround 1 to 3 inches for most areas above that elevation, however \nthe most likely spot will be the Kaibab Plateau where 2 to 4 inches \nseem probable. Model soundings do show the potential for a rain/snow \nmix down to around 7000 feet or so, but no accumulations are \nexpected at this elevation given that surface temperatures look to \nremain above freezing. Some elevated surfaces (bridges, overpasses) \nmay be slick early Saturday.\n\nWinds are also expected to increase downwind of the higher terrain \novernight as the upper-level jet increases. Coverage of the winds \nlooks to be limited given that much of the area will decouple, aside \nfrom the downslope areas, where gusts upwards of 30-40 mph will be \npossible. During the day Saturday, some gusty winds will be possible \nover a more broad area, but will likely not be as strong given the \nsubsiding jet overhead.\n\nSunday through Thursday...Dry conditions set in for Sunday and \nMonday as high pressure briefly builds back into the region. This is \nshort-lived however as unsettled conditions are expected to return \nlate Monday into Tuesday as a trough lifts through Arizona. \nUncertainty remains in the exact track of this system with guidance \nsplit between a further north or southward track of the vort max. If \nthe more southerly track wins out, we can expect a colder and wetter \noutcome vs the northward track where we remain in the warm sector \nand with less forcing. Drier conditions set in for Wednesday and \nThursday, however unsettled conditions look to potentially return \nonce again late next week.",
